{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"record_version|schema.*counsel|counsel.schema\\\" attavo/records.py tests/test_types_mirror_schemas.py tests/test_adapters.py tests/test_adapters_codex.py; sed -n '1,240p' attavo/records.py; git diff 2a6fc89 -- docs attavo.toml | sed -n '1,260p'; git diff --check 512d963a..12bb329\"","aggregated_output":"tests/test_adapters_codex.py:503:def test_codex_counsel_schema_requires_the_structured_answer(tmp_path: Path) -> None:\ntests/test_adapters.py:742:def test_claude_code_counsel_schema_requires_the_structured_answer(tmp_path: Path) -> None:\ntests/test_types_mirror_schemas.py:27:    \"CounselRecord\": \"records/counsel.schema.json\",\nattavo/records.py:42:    into a dict (issue #60, acceptance 6). Defaults record_version to 1\nattavo/records.py:47:    data.setdefault(\"record_version\", 1)\nattavo/records.py:121:    \"\"\"Every record gains record_version (this stage schema's own $id\nattavo/records.py:146:    record = {**record, \"record_version\": schema_id_version(schema), \"item\": item,\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport re\nfrom collections.abc import Mapping\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any, Literal, cast, overload\n\nfrom attavo.common import PRODUCT_ROOT, product_version\nfrom attavo.types import ApprovalRecord, BuildRecord, CounselRecord, SpecRecord, VerifyRecord\n\n# --- record files: <stage>-NN.json, one per attempt, never overwritten ---\n\nSCHEMA_RECORDS_DIR = PRODUCT_ROOT / \"schema\" / \"records\"\n\n\ndef stage_schema(stage: str) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"schema/records/<stage>.schema.json -- this repository's own\n    declaration of that stage's record shape (issue #60). The only\n    artifact the outbound contract needs; there is no accessor for it\n    beyond reading the file.\"\"\"\n    schema: dict[str, Any] = json.loads((SCHEMA_RECORDS_DIR / f\"{stage}.schema.json\").read_text())\n    return schema\n\n\ndef schema_id_version(schema: dict[str, Any]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"The trailing integer of a schema's $id (\".../records/build/2\" -> 2)\n    -- the single place a record shape's version lives. write_record reads\n    this at call time rather than carrying its own copy, so the version it\n    stamps cannot drift from what the schema declares (issue #60).\"\"\"\n    return int(schema[\"$id\"].rsplit(\"/\", 1)[-1])\n\n\nclass RecordItemMismatch(RuntimeError):\n    \"\"\"A record's item field disagrees with the directory it was read\n    from (issue #60, acceptance 5) -- a misfiled or copied record, refused\n    rather than trusted.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef _load_record(path: Path, item_id: str) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"The one place in this repository that turns a record file's bytes\n    into a dict (issue #60, acceptance 6). Defaults record_version to 1\n    when absent -- version 1 is every record written before this release\n    and is never migrated -- and fills item from the directory when\n    absent; refuses when a present item disagrees with it.\"\"\"\n    data = json.loads(path.read_text())\n    data.setdefault(\"record_version\", 1)\n    item = data.get(\"item\")\n    if item is None:\n        data[\"item\"] = item_id\n    elif item != item_id:\n        raise RecordItemMismatch(\n            f\"{path}: item {item!r} disagrees with its directory {item_id!r}\")\n    return cast(dict[str, Any], data)\n\n\n# The typed loaders: a literal stage name selects the record kind the caller\n# gets back (issue #189). The record is read from a file and not validated\n# here, so the kind is the schema's promise, the same one the write side\n# already keeps (write_record refuses a key its schema does not declare);\n# a stage held in a variable is the untyped fallback.\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"spec\"]) -> dict[int, SpecRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"approval\"]) -> dict[int, ApprovalRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"build\"]) -> dict[int, BuildRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"verify\"]) -> dict[int, VerifyRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"counsel\"]) -> dict[int, CounselRecord]: ...\n@overload\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> dict[int, Any]: ...\ndef parse_attempts(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> dict[int, Any]:\n    attempts: dict[int, Any] = {}\n    if not records_dir.is_dir():\n        return attempts\n    for f in records_dir.glob(f\"{stage}-*.json\"):\n        m = RECORD_RE.match(f.name)\n        if not m or m.group(1) != stage:\n            continue\n        try:\n            attempts[int(m.group(2))] = _load_record(f, records_dir.name)\n        except json.JSONDecodeError:\n            continue\n    return attempts\n\n\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"spec\"]) -> SpecRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"approval\"]) -> ApprovalRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"build\"]) -> BuildRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"verify\"]) -> VerifyRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: Literal[\"counsel\"]) -> CounselRecord | None: ...\n@overload\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> Any | None: ...\ndef latest_record(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> Any:\n    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)\n    return attempts[max(attempts)] if attempts else None\n\n\ndef next_attempt_number(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> int:\n    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)\n    return (max(attempts) + 1) if attempts else 1\n\n\ndef latest_attempt_path(records_dir: Path, stage: str) -> Path | None:\n    \"\"\"The path of the highest-numbered <stage>-NN.json, or None.\"\"\"\n    attempts = parse_attempts(records_dir, stage)\n    if not attempts:\n        return None\n    return records_dir / f\"{stage}-{max(attempts):02d}.json\"\n\n\ndef write_record(records_dir: Path, stage: str, record: Mapping[str, Any], m: dict[str, Any],\n                 item: str, log: str | None = None) -> Path:\n    \"\"\"Every record gains record_version (this stage schema's own $id\n    version -- issue #60) and item (verbatim from the caller, matching the\n    record's directory segment), plus product_version (what ran, derived\n    here) and product_pin (what the tenant declared, copied verbatim from\n    the manifest, or null when the manifest carries no [attavo] table) --\n    the latter two recorded, never enforced (issue #49). A manifest\n    missing the table is an absent fact, not a crash: this is the one\n    caller of write_record that must not raise on a shape the manifest\n    schema forbids but tomllib.load happily returns.\n\n    Refuses (raises ValueError) a record carrying a key this stage's\n    schema does not declare -- the declared key set enforced with the\n    standard library, not a jsonschema import: bin/attavo is a single\n    script a tenant pins, and a runtime dependency changes installation.\n\n    Also refuses a `status: \"failed\"` record that carries no trail at all\n    -- log the empty or missing text it will sit beside, and refusal and\n    halted (where the stage's schema has them) both null (issue #53).\n    `returned`, `pass`, `halted`, and `refused` owe no such trail. log is\n    None for a stage that invoked no adapter (verify's `returned`), which\n    write_record then writes no log file for; otherwise the log is\n    written before the record, so a refusal leaves no half-pair.\"\"\"\n    records_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    nn = next_attempt_number(records_dir, stage)\n    schema = stage_schema(stage)\n    record = {**record, \"record_version\": schema_id_version(schema), \"item\": item,\n              \"product_version\": product_version(),\n              \"product_pin\": (m.get(\"attavo\") or {}).get(\"version\")}\n    undeclared = set(record) - set(schema.get(\"properties\", {}))\n    if undeclared:\n        raise ValueError(f\"write_record: {stage} record carries key(s) its schema does not \"\n                         f\"declare: {', '.join(sorted(undeclared))}\")\n    if (record.get(\"status\") == \"failed\" and not log\n            and record.get(\"refusal\") is None and record.get(\"halted\") is None):\n        raise ValueError(f\"write_record: {stage} record status is 'failed' with no log, \"\n                         f\"refusal, or halted to say why\")\n    path = records_dir / f\"{stage}-{nn:02d}.json\"\n    if log is not None:\n        path.with_suffix(\".log\").write_text(log)\n    path.write_text(json.dumps(record, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\n    return path\n\n\nRECORD_RE = re.compile(r\"^([a-z]+)-([0-9]{2,})\\.json$\")\n\n\ndef load_records(records_dir: Path) -> dict[str, dict[str, dict[str, Any]]]:\n    \"\"\"{item_id: {stage: latest_record}} for every <records>/<item>/<stage>-NN.json.\"\"\"\n    items: dict[str, dict[str, dict[str, Any]]] = {}\n    if not records_dir.is_dir():\n        return items\n    for item_dir in sorted(p for p in records_dir.iterdir() if p.is_dir()):\n        by_stage: dict[str, dict[int, dict[str, Any]]] = {}\n        for rec in sorted(item_dir.glob(\"*.json\")):\n            m = RECORD_RE.match(rec.name)\n            if not m:\n                continue\n            stage, n = m.group(1), int(m.group(2))\n            try:\n                data = _load_record(rec, item_dir.name)\n            except json.JSONDecodeError:\n                continue\n            by_stage.setdefault(stage, {})[n] = data\n        items[item_dir.name] = {stage: attempts[max(attempts)] for stage, attempts in by_stage.items()}\n    return items\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-l1fsXchS' (errno=Operation not permitted)\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-ChO6wGbW' (errno=Operation not permitted)\ndiff --git a/attavo.toml b/attavo.toml\nindex 72b8db8..e5d79e8 100644\n--- a/attavo.toml\n+++ b/attavo.toml\n@@ -67,3 +67,6 @@ ceiling_s = 3600\n \n [drive.verify]\n ceiling_s = 1200\n+\n+[counsel]\n+reads = [\"docs/method-rules.md\", \"docs/counsel-rulings.md\", \"docs/r3-rulings.md\", \"docs/storage-ruling.md\"]\ndiff --git a/docs/README.md b/docs/README.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..7841d80\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/README.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,3 @@\n+# docs/: what counsel reads\n+\n+The method rules, briefs and rulings that govern this repository as its own tenant, at the version the manifest's `[counsel].reads` names. Counsel is handed these paths before it answers (`ATTAVO_COUNSEL_READS`); a path named in the manifest that does not exist is refused, not skipped. The living copies of the operator's documents are kept elsewhere; what is here is what a record may cite, and a citation into `docs/` resolves to a file in the repository at the record's commit.\ndiff --git a/docs/counsel-rulings.md b/docs/counsel-rulings.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..2c7c8ab\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/counsel-rulings.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@\n+# Counsel and the conversation: rulings of 2026-09-25\n+\n+Observed on #219 (R3's own build): attempt 1 halted after 3,510 s as `non-question`; `counsel-01` read it, verdict `answer`, text \"Continue the build from the existing working tree\"; under R2 the product filed an issue and waited for the operator. Under R3 counsel's `answer` is the next attempt's steer.\n+\n+## Troubleshoot, measured\n+\n+One `attavo counsel 219 --ask \"why did this build stop?\"` in a scratch checkout: 73 s, three model turns. Turn one answered with no citations; the adapter's citation guard sent it back; it read the record, the last 30 KB of the 4.8 MB build log, searched for the brief and found nothing (the corpus was not in the repository); turn three answered with citations. It wrote `counsel-02.json` into the scratch checkout, which was discarded.\n+\n+## Ruled\n+\n+1. A question to counsel is a follow-up to a stop counsel has already read. `attavo counsel N --ask` is handed the prior counsel records for the item as its first input (verdict, text, citations), never re-derives a class a record states, and writes its answer as the next `counsel-NN.json` with `answers` naming the counsel record it follows beside `record`. The screen commits and pushes it like any counsel record. The answer is a record: an intervention in the attestation and precedent.\n+2. The panel's first message is the newest counsel record read from the branch (no invocation); `--ask` runs only for a question the operator types.\n+3. Resuming a vendor's own thread is an adapter-internal optimisation, recorded in the adapter's report when used, never part of the contract; the records are the mechanism that carries context between invocations.\n+4. For a level that is the operator's (budget spent, two identical failures, counsel says `person`) the panel opens with the exhaustion report and ends in one of three drafted acts, each a record: a ruling (R4), a spec amendment (moves the approval), a product or tenant defect (counsel's `patch`).\n+5. Counsel is handed the stop record, the signature and the stage's last events rather than the whole log, unless the question is about the log.\n+6. Counsel's corpus, the text it reads before it answers (method rules, briefs, rulings), lives in the repository under `docs/`, named by `[counsel].reads`; a manifest naming a path that does not exist is refused.\n+\n+## The conversation and its records\n+\n+The exchange is split from its outcome. A turn in the conversation is working material and lives in the product's datastore with the item; a record is written only for an outcome the attestation cares about: counsel's verdict on a stop (answer, patch, person); a follow-up whose answer changes what the next attempt is told; or an operator turning a turn into an act (a ruling, a spec amendment, an issue). The record carries the question that produced it. A question that belongs to Plan is answered and routed there; in Execute the only outcomes are those above, so a tangent produces nothing in git however long it runs.\ndiff --git a/docs/method-rules.md b/docs/method-rules.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..93d0d2e\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/method-rules.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,57 @@\n+# Method rules: reads that lie, and what a session owes before it writes\n+\n+Carried from the pilot's document index on 2026-09-03, stripped of the pilot's specimens. These are the rules the reviewing session kept as habit and broke when tired; in Attest they are the seed of the challenge's answer contract (Phase 3): an answer that does not satisfy them is refused by a script, not by memory. Each rule was earned by at least one specimen in the pilot.\n+\n+**The one rule that covers all of them: a read taken while something is in flight is stale by construction.** Report it as a timestamped observation, never as state.\n+\n+1. **Directory listings are served from a cache.**\n+2. **A read taken while a session is mid-command** describes a moment that has passed.\n+3. **GitHub's API is eventually consistent.**\n+4. **A truncated listing is not evidence of absence.**\n+5. **A file transfer tool's success message is not delivery.**\n+6. **A ruling recorded in a project doc is not repository state.**\n+7. **Local `main` is stale after any merge until the operator pulls.** Branch from `origin/main`. An aborted pull leaves it stale for the rest of the session; a three-dot diff against it then attributes another lane's merged work to the branch under review.\n+8. **A lane branch's internal commits are checked by nobody.** The PR's checks are the truth.\n+9. **An operator-side project doc is not a citable source for an append-only repo record.** **Nor is a live URL.**\n+10. **Branch currency is verified at review time, never asserted from memory.**\n+11. **A required status check satisfied by \"skipped\" is not a check that ran.**\n+12. **A failed `git checkout` leaves the next command acting on the wrong ref.**\n+13. **The absence of a subcommand is not evidence the capability is missing.** Search the repository's scripts, commands, workflows and templates first.\n+14. **A red check may be an observation about the environment, not a finding about the branch.**\n+15. **An aggregated API row is not attribution.**\n+16. **A number without its counting method is not reproducible.**\n+17. **A local run of a check and CI's run of it are different programs.** Ask which program produced a red before diagnosing it.\n+18. **A red that appears only locally is a claim about the invocation, not the branch**, and the reverse: a red that appears only in CI is a claim about the environment the check named.\n+19. **Two workflows sharing a `name:` share `${{ github.workflow }}`.** Key concurrency on the filename; the check name in `gh pr checks` is the only way to tell which failed.\n+20. **A gate that is configured is not a gate that runs.** Read a check's first ten lines for the early return before citing it as enforcing. **And before authorizing any write, enumerate the sibling guards that read the same artifact.**\n+21. **A *successful* checkout is the more dangerous one.** It exits zero and moves a running session's HEAD silently. Tagging and merging need no working tree; a second lane gets a worktree.\n+22. **A CLI message, error or success, is not a statement about state.** `gh pr view <n> --json mergeable,mergeStateStatus,reviewDecision,statusCheckRollup`; for merges, `--json state,mergedAt,mergeCommit`. **A `gh run list --limit 1` after a push returns the run that push started.** Select a run by the sha it ran on, never by \"latest.\"\n+23. **A gate correct within its own field of view may not reach what it protects** (the seam class: a check that is right about its inputs and blind to the thing next to them).\n+24. **The file in the tree is not the program the agent is running.**\n+25. **A grep probe's tokenization is part of its claim.** Zero-hit results from an automated probe are re-checked by hand. **A carrier claim requires the executable line that reads the value.**\n+26. **A reachability claim is only as good as its fetched history and its stated `merge-base --is-ancestor` argument order.**\n+27. **A shallow marker in the shared `.git` lies to every session and subagent at once.** Check `git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository` first.\n+28. **A register candidate is not a citable fact.** A finding number is citable only after the findings register holds it.\n+29. **A signal field is a reading of its producer, not of the world.** Read the function that computed it.\n+30. **A pipeline verdict and a definition-of-done verdict are two readers, not one state.**\n+31. **An issue number recorded in the merged register is a repo read, not a forge read.**\n+32. **A passing shape check is not evidence the value is usable.** **Before citing a check as proof a field is correct, read the code that consumes the field.**\n+33. **An API error is not an empty result set.** **Every probe asserts its own success before its result is read**, never `.get()` into a default. And every probe in a chain guards its own value with `test -n` before the next command reads it; a null carried forward exits zero.\n+34. **An identifier is not a reference until it resolves.** **Before writing an identifier into an artifact another session will act on, resolve it: to an issue, a sha, or a file path.**\n+35. **A negative search inherits the scope of the corpus searched.** Absence in a subtree is not absence in the repository.\n+36. **A check's answer is about a commit, not a PR.**\n+37. **A gate's failure count is not its rejection count.**\n+38. **A tracker search immediately after a create describes a moment before the create.** The number of a new issue is read from the URL its `create` printed, never from a search for its title.\n+39. **A command block handed over for pasting contains no placeholder.** Either the value is derived inside the block, or the block is split so the value is read first and typed into the second.\n+40. **`pytest --deselect` matches by nodeid prefix, not by exact string**, so a deselect line silently disables every test whose name extends the deselected one. The read that catches it is counting \"N deselected\" against the number of `--deselect` flags actually passed. The fix is renaming the new test, never widening the deselect.\n+41. **An absence is a claim, and a claim needs its own read.** Reading a writer and its outputs never establishes that no declaration exists; only looking where a declaration would live does.\n+42. **A fix that moves the fact to where the write is allowed, instead of moving the write to where the fact is known, is the pattern that made the pilot unusable.** Ask first: at what moment is this fact known, and what mechanism already stands there? Four tells, three of them countable: the fix adds a step, run, or commit to every item's traversal (the per-item counts in the records must not rise); it writes a fact after the event it describes, in a second commit; it ships with its own recovery path; its checks can reject nothing. The fourth is prose until the screen makes it a number: the fix has no consumer beyond this item, this tenant, this week. A one-time exception for the item in hand is how the pattern starts, and is refused on the same ground.\n+\n+**Git object reads against a local ref are trustworthy. Directory listings, anything remote, anything concurrent, and anything aggregated are not.**\n+\n+## The four the answer contract enforces first\n+\n+- **Enumerate the sibling guards.** Before authorizing any write, name every mechanism that reads the same artifact: the instruction that asks for it, the mechanism that performs it, the guard that judges it.\n+- **Resolve every identifier.** An identifier is not a reference until it resolves to an issue, a sha, or a file.\n+- **A gate's failure count is not its rejection count.** Read what was rejected and on how many distinct pieces of work before calling a gate earned.\n+- **State the per-item cost of every fix, before and after.** Steps, runs, and commits per traversal, from the records. A fix whose after is larger than its before is not authorized without a ruling that names why (rule 42).\ndiff --git a/docs/r3-rulings.md b/docs/r3-rulings.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..d9769ac\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/r3-rulings.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,17 @@\n+# R3 (retries, bounds and budgets): the nine rulings of 2026-09-25\n+\n+All nine proposed by the session and ruled, agreed, by the operator on 2026-09-25 with the session's recommendations adopted. They sit on top of ruling C and its two amendments in the reconciler brief (2026-09-20). Item #219 carries the spec; v0.28.0 carries the code.\n+\n+1. **Failure signature.** `stop.signature` on every non-pass stop, compared by string equality, runs of digits removed on every family: last event line (died, hung); adapter's stop detail (transient, others); sorted names of red required checks (returned check); sorted titles of blocking findings (returned review). The exhaustion report prints signatures side by side; a signature too fine is the known failure mode and the side-by-side is how it is seen.\n+2. **What a returned retry carries.** The red check's log via `[forge].check_log_command`, or the review's blocking findings. Collapsed with 3 into one input.\n+3. **How a steer reaches the next attempt.** The existing `ATTAVO_FEEDBACK` file, seeded on the retried attempt's first iteration with class and signature, the evidence, and the newest steer newer than the blocking record (counsel's `answer` now, an operator's `/rule` after R4). The record carries `retry_of` and `steer`. No new adapter input.\n+4. **Transient wait.** `[drive].retry_after_s`, default 300; died and hung retry on the next pass with no wait.\n+5. **Ceilings.** `[drive.build].ceiling_s` and `[drive.verify].ceiling_s`, read against the corpus of build wall clocks at each pin; past it, terminated and recorded hung with `stop.bound = \"ceiling\"`; non-streaming adapters (`conformance.json` `streams_events: false`) are judged by the ceiling alone; the doctor refuses a `[drive]` table without both ceilings.\n+6. **Budget accounting.** Every marker counts, recovered attempts included; the report always says attempt N of the budget.\n+7. **`--again`.** Resets a spent budget only, recorded in the marker; a no-op inside the budget; retired in R4.\n+8. **Environment refusal.** Recorded in R3, never retried per item; acted on by R4's tenant-wide breaker.\n+9. **Exhaustion report's home.** `attavo drive N --report` (state stopped, `attempts`, `signatures_match`, `rulings_available`); no new command.\n+\n+Found while writing the spec: a review with findings passed whatever their severity (severity is free text); the returned-review family needed a definition, so a finding of severity `blocking` fails the review as `returned` and every other severity is filed as before. R2 as merged reads alive from the heartbeat and never calls a heartbeated worker hung by event age; the ceiling is therefore the only wall-clock bound a worker has, which is what ruling 5 intended.\n+\n+Exclusions: no cross-executor retry; no backoff beyond the one fixed wait.\ndiff --git a/docs/storage-ruling.md b/docs/storage-ruling.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..5164aaf\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/storage-ruling.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@\n+# The three stores: where a thing lives (ruled 2026-09-25)\n+\n+One test decides the store: does the attestation bind to it?\n+\n+1. **Git**: facts and policy the attestation binds to. The spec and its criteria; approval bound to the spec's sha; invocation markers; build, review, counsel and ruling records; the manifest, including policy such as `[operators].approvers` (a claim the attestation makes, so a tenant must be able to prove it with the repository alone). Small, versioned, travels with the branch, survives the product.\n+2. **The product's datastore**: state and working material the attestation does not bind to. Identity state; indexes over git (ledger, measurements, report caches, legitimate only as caches rebuilt from git); the Plan area's working documents; the per-item conversation. A Plan document a spec's research record or a ruling cites is copied into `docs/` in the tenant at its cited version in the same commit as the citation.\n+3. **Content-addressed bulk**: bytes a fact points at by hash: build logs, events files, later any executor evidence. The record carries `log_sha256` and a locator; the attestation binds to the hash. The backend is the tenant's declaration in the manifest, offered in this order: git LFS (keeps \"verify with the repository alone\" true), the tenant's own object store behind a `put`/`get` command pair, GitHub release assets. GitHub Actions artifacts are not a candidate: they expire.\n+\n+## Consequences\n+\n+- Records are never moved to a datastore to save repository size; they are not the size.\n+- First step: every build, verify and counsel record carries `log_sha256`, computed at write time, with the log still in git; one field that turns every later move into a change of locator, not of attestation. Beside it, two measurements at retire: repository size per item and log bytes per attempt; they say when to declare a bulk backend.\n+- The index over git is built when a doctor or report read is slow enough to move a decision.\n+- Counsel's corpus belongs in the repository under `docs/`; `[counsel].reads` names it.\n+- Auth and RBAC: policy in git (the manifest), identity delegated to the forge, state in the datastore.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-MmGaJDdh' (errno=Operation not permitted)\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-BzUxlyJB' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
